      "dom_id": "step-q339383",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A pharmaceutical mixer initially holds $300\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved medication is pumped into the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of medication $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the mixer $t$ minutes after pumping begins?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $Q$ be the amount of medication in the mixer at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which medication enters the mixer minus the rate at which medication leaves the mixer.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the mixer.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the medication enters the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *medication* enters the mixer is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the mixer.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the mixer remains $300\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of medication in the mixer, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *medication* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the mixer initially contains $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =1 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$",
