    "q-339403": {
      "dom_id": "step-q339403",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A wastewater treatment basin initially holds $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved neutralizing agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent is pumped into the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $Q$ be the amount of agent in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which agent enters the tank minus the rate at which agent leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which agent enters the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent enters the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *agent* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which agent exits the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which agent leaves the basin, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $8\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+8t-4t=(320+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of agent in the basin, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min},$ *agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(80+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|} \\\\  & =80+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=80+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(80+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q) & =(80+t)\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (80+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.4(80+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t)) & =0.4(80+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(80+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(80+t) & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(80+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)+\\frac{C}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2.2,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2.2 \\\\ 0.2(80+0)+\\frac{C}{80+0} & =2.2 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{80} & =2.2 \\\\ \\frac{C}{80} & =-13.8 \\\\ C & =-1104\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)-\\frac{1104}{80+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(5) & =0.2(80+5)-\\frac{1104}{80+5} \\\\  & =17-\\frac{1104}{85} \\\\  & ≈4.01\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
